From 453858e23e85f8e222e9189d1b86646d308a9f8f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 02:49:49 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 15/18] WebGL: draw a page's scene once per eye from its own
 camera

The camera hack (webgl.stereo-camera.enabled, off by default; the
edition's package turns it on): a page that draws a 3D scene with WebGL
and no WebXR is shown in stereo, one world seen by two cameras, without
any change to the page.

The camera is recognised when the page uploads it (WebGLStereoCamera.h):
a mat4, a plain uniform or a member of a uniform block, that is a
perspective projection (its last row (0, 0, -1, 0), or (0, 0, 1, 0) for
a left-handed one) or a view-projection product (its last row a unit
vector), with a positive near distance. Matrices whose names say they
belong to a light or a shadow are left alone. An orthographic camera
(2D, a HUD) has no such row and stays at the screen's depth.

Each draw into the canvas's own framebuffer is then made twice, the left
eye into the canvas and the right eye into a second framebuffer of the
same size, each with its eye's camera: parallel axes, frustums shifted
off-axis to share one window at the zero-parallax distance C (5 times
the near distance, webgl.stereo-camera.convergence), never toed in. In
clip space that is x' = x + s(w - C), s = -/+ half the separation (2 %
of the view's width, webgl.stereo-camera.separation). Clears go to both
eyes. Draws into the page's own framebuffers (shadow maps, render
targets) and with transform feedback are made once. A uniform block
holding the camera is rewritten for each eye from a copy of the page's
data (buffers up to 64 KiB) and restored.

The canvas then presents both eyes full side by side, left eye first,
twice its width (StereoSource::CAMERA: the place shows one view, as for
WebXR), and is declared on its own surface. It does so only where the
two views can be declared: on Wayland, with WebRender's native
compositor (pictures on surfaces of their own), and a compositor that
takes declared stereo content (kde_stereo_content_manager_v1 among the
globals; gfxVars::StereoContentDeclaration). Anywhere else the canvas
stays as the page drew it. A canvas in a WebXR session keeps its
session's views. MOZ_LOG=WebGLStereo:4 logs each present's draws per eye
and into the page's framebuffers.

A scene drawn into the page's own render target and then copied to the
canvas (post-processing) is drawn once and stays flat.

